From 11644146c7fd86c11b5dbaffd6fa989bda1e0c8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 21:03:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] camera: Mode Ladder, the FPS picks the resolution and
 compression

Why: Patrik shoots 4K Clear HDR 16-bit up to 25 fps and 4K SDR 12-bit
above it, up to the build maximum of 50. Switching by hand between the
two modes for every FPS change is slow and easy to get wrong.

What: an ordered list of resolutions (sensor mode, compression, optional
"Up to" FPS) in ~/.config/apertar-core/mode_ladder.conf. While the ladder
is on, a set_fps outside a take moves to the first resolution that
reaches the FPS, with one apply; in a take the running resolution's top
caps set_fps, ramp_fps and the speed-ramp stepper. The pure logic is in
src/camera/ModeLadder.hpp (host-tested). New commands get_mode_ladder and
set_mode_ladder (rungs, enabled, dry_run); set_resolution,
set_raw_bit_depth, set_clear_hdr and the compression setters are refused
while the ladder picks the mode. New state fields fps_take_max,
ladder_enabled, ladder_active, ladder_rung, ladder_count, ladder_text and
ladder_tops in all three writers. Favorites that name another mode are
refused; hardware resolution inputs do nothing while the ladder is on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T93jucKMhWYnQM1yyFVkbH
